ARCHITECTURE

phantom-auth is a FastAPI service that terminates user authentication
in front of phantom-daemon. Every dashboard request from the SPA hits
auth-service first; only authenticated, role-checked requests reach
the daemon over its Unix domain socket.


Position in the stack
---------------------

  Browser (dashboard SPA)
       │
       │  HTTPS
       ▼
  nginx (TLS termination)
       │
       │  HTTP
       ▼
  auth-service ── /auth/*  login, MFA, sessions, users, audit
               ── /api/*   authenticated reverse proxy to daemon
       │
       │  UDS (unix:///var/run/phantom/daemon.sock)
       ▼
  phantom-daemon

  No daemon endpoint is reachable without a valid auth-service
  token. The daemon does not implement authentication itself.


Process model
-------------

  Single Python process, FastAPI under uvicorn.

  All long-lived state lives on app.state, populated by the lifespan
  context manager (auth_service/app.py:lifespan) in five phases:

    1. Load configuration from environment      (config.py)
    2. Load Ed25519 signing keypair from disk   (crypto/keys.py)
    3. Validate auth.db exists                  (db/repository.py)
    4. Build httpx client to daemon (UDS or HTTP)
    5. Build in-memory rate limiter             (middleware/rate_limit.py)

  Startup failure is critical and fatal: sys.exit(1). The service
  refuses to run with partially-initialised state.


Persistence layer
-----------------

  A single SQLite file at $AUTH_DB_DIR/auth.db, opened with
  PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL, wal_autocheckpoint = 1 and
  foreign_keys = ON.

  Schema (auth_service/db/schema.sql):

    users                id, username, password_hash, role,
                         totp_secret, timestamps.
    totp_backup_codes    per-user one-time backup codes (hashed).
    sessions             id, user_id, token_jti, token_hash,
                         issued_at, expires_at, last_activity_at,
                         revoked.
    audit_log            what an identified user does: logins, MFA
                         steps, password changes, user management
                         and proxy requests. Append-only. user_id
                         holds who acted (Audit log). A login
                         refused before the user is known goes to
                         the service log instead (Service log).

  The file is created once by tools/bootstrap.py during initial
  setup. If the file is missing, startup aborts. Every connection
  the runtime opens (db/connection.py) runs schema.sql, which is
  CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS throughout, and one forward migration:
  the role column on users. A file removed while the service runs
  comes back empty on the next request, and no user can log in.

  Connections are per-request, not pooled. _DBMiddleware
  (auth_service/app.py) opens a fresh sqlite3 connection on every
  inbound request, stores it on request.state.db, and closes it
  after the response. This eliminates the threading hazards that
  arise from sharing a single sqlite3 connection across asyncio
  workers — a real bug fixed in v1.1.0.


Authentication flow
-------------------

  1. POST /auth/login            username + password → either:
                                   - mfa_required with a short-lived
                                     MFA token (120s default) when
                                     TOTP is enrolled, or
                                   - access token + session record.
                                 Both branches write to audit_log.

  2. POST /auth/mfa/verify       MFA token + TOTP code → access
                                 token + session.

  3. POST /auth/totp/backup      MFA token + backup code → access
                                 token. Backup code is consumed.

  4. POST /auth/logout           Revokes the active session by JTI.

  5. GET  /auth/me               Returns current user + role.

  Access tokens default to 24h. Sessions also enforce a 30-minute
  inactivity timeout — every authenticated call updates
  last_activity_at; idle sessions auto-revoke on the next access
  attempt.


Authorization
-------------

  middleware/auth.py:require_auth
  Steps:

    1. Extract Bearer token from the Authorization header.
    2. Verify Ed25519 signature, decode payload.
    3. Check that the typ claim is "access".
    4. Look up the session by jti; reject if missing or revoked.
    5. Check that stored token_hash matches sha256 of the raw token
       — prevents reuse if a JTI leaks but the token does not.
    6. Check inactivity timeout; revoke if exceeded.
    7. Update last_activity_at.

  Two roles. On the /auth/* routes that take a token:

    Method  Route                        Target      admin  superadmin
    ──────  ───────────────────────────  ──────────  ─────  ──────────
    GET     /auth/me                     self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/logout                 self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/password/verify        self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/password/change        self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/totp/setup             self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/totp/confirm           self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/totp/disable           self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/totp/disable           other user  no     yes
    POST    /auth/users/{name}/password  self        yes    yes
    POST    /auth/users/{name}/password  other user  no     yes
    GET     /auth/users                  -           no     yes
    POST    /auth/users                  -           no     yes
    DELETE  /auth/users/{name}           other user  no     yes
    GET     /auth/audit                  -           no     yes

  /auth/login, /auth/mfa/verify and /auth/totp/backup take no token
  (Authentication flow). On the /api/* proxy, admin holds the VIEW
  and TUNNEL routes and superadmin every route: the table is under
  Reverse proxy.

  require_superadmin, a thin wrapper around require_auth that asserts
  payload.role == "superadmin", guards listing, creating and deleting
  users and /auth/audit. users.py and totp.py check the role inline
  when the target is another user. The proxy checks GRANTS.


Cryptography
------------

  JWT — Ed25519, manual implementation (crypto/jwt.py)
    No extra dependency. Header.Payload.Signature with base64url
    parts. Algorithm is pinned to "EdDSA"; any other alg in the
    header fails verification.

    The signing keypair lives on disk as two 64-char hex files in
    $AUTH_SECRETS_DIR (mounted as Docker secrets in production):
    one for the private (signing) key, one for the public (verify)
    key. Both are length- and format-validated at load time.

  Passwords — Argon2id (crypto/password.py via PyNaCl)
    PyNaCl's default limits, OPSLIMIT_INTERACTIVE and
    MEMLIMIT_INTERACTIVE (2 passes, 64 MiB). Hash + verify only; no
    parameter tuning is exposed.

  TOTP — RFC 6238, standard library (crypto/totp.py)
    HMAC-SHA1, 6 digits, 30-second period, one period of drift on
    either side, 160-bit secret. Emits otpauth:// URIs for
    authenticator-app enrollment. Backup codes are 8 codes of 8
    characters each, stored as SHA-256 hashes (one-time use).


Reverse proxy
-------------

  routes/proxy.py owns /api/{path:path} and forwards the daemon
  routes in its ROUTES table, matched exactly on method and path. A
  request outside the table does not reach the daemon, and the URL
  sent to the daemon is the table's path: a path spelled another way
  has no row.

  Each row names the access the route needs, and GRANTS maps an
  access to the roles that hold it. Paths are under /api/:

    Method  Route                       Access    admin  superadmin
    ──────  ──────────────────────────  ────────  ─────  ──────────
    GET     core/hello                  VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     core/wireguard              VIEW      yes    yes
    POST    core/wireguard/peer         VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     core/firewall/groups/list   VIEW      yes    yes
    POST    core/firewall/groups/get    VIEW      yes    yes
    POST    core/firewall/routing/list  VIEW      yes    yes
    POST    core/firewall/rules/list    VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     core/firewall/table         VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     core/network                VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     core/network/validate       VIEW      yes    yes
    POST    dns/get                     VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     core/clients/list           VIEW      yes    yes
    GET     multihop/status             VIEW      yes    yes
    POST    core/clients/assign         TUNNEL    yes    yes
    POST    core/clients/get            TUNNEL    yes    yes
    POST    core/clients/config         TUNNEL    yes    yes
    POST    core/clients/revoke         TUNNEL    yes    yes
    POST    core/network/cidr           OPERATE   no     yes
    POST    dns/change                  OPERATE   no     yes
    GET     multihop/list               MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    multihop/config             MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    multihop/import             MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    multihop/update             MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    multihop/enable             MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    multihop/disable            MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    multihop/remove             MULTIHOP  no     yes
    POST    core/backup/export          CUSTODY   no     yes
    POST    core/backup/import          CUSTODY   no     yes

  What each access covers:

    VIEW       reads state and carries no private key
    TUNNEL     creates, reads or removes a client tunnel together with its keys
    OPERATE    changes the server configuration
    MULTIHOP   lists, reads or changes the multihop exits together with their keys
    CUSTODY    exports or imports the whole data set together with every client key

  Per-request flow:
    1. Apply require_auth (unauthenticated requests never reach the
       daemon).
    2. Take the audit actor from require_auth (request.state.actor).
    3. Look up method and path in ROUTES; 404 NOT_FOUND outside the
       table, audited.
    4. Check the token's role against GRANTS; 403
       SUPERADMIN_REQUIRED if it is missing, audited, before the
       body is read.
    5. Read the request body; reject with 413 if larger than the
       route's limit (AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY, or
       AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY on backup).
    6. Strip Host / Authorization / Content-Length /
       Transfer-Encoding from forwarded headers.
    7. Forward via httpx AsyncClient (UDS transport in production).
    8. Audit-log the request: method, path, query, response status.
    9. Pass the response back, preserving content-type and
       stripping Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding and
       Content-Encoding (uvicorn re-computes them).
   10. JSON responses are re-encoded as JSONResponse; other
       responses, the backup export tarball among them, pass
       through raw.


Rate limiting
-------------

  middleware/rate_limit.py:RateLimiter — in-memory sliding window
  per source IP, threadsafe via a single Lock. Default: 5 attempts
  per 60 seconds. One budget per IP covers /auth/login,
  /auth/mfa/verify and /auth/totp/backup; the rest of the API relies
  on the session/token model.

  The counter resets only when an access token is issued. With TOTP
  enrolled, a correct password alone does not reset it.


Audit log
---------

  user_id names who acted, written once with the row
  (audit.py:account):

    <id>-<name>        an account: its 32-character id, a dash and
                       its name. The name stays with the row after
                       the account is deleted.
    system:<source>    a writer with no account

  require_auth puts the actor of an authenticated request in
  request.state.actor. A row about another account (user management,
  a password reset, turning off its TOTP) holds that account in
  detail.target, in the same form; detail repeats no other name.

  GET /auth/audit splits user_id into user_id and username, and the
  username filter matches that name. A row written before 1.3.0
  holds a bare account id: its name comes from the users table, and
  in user_created, user_deleted, totp_disabled and a password reset
  that id is the target account.


Service log
-----------

  The service writes its own lines to stdout through the logger
  phantom-auth: time, level, logger name, message
  (__main__.py:log_config). uvicorn writes no access log; nginx
  logs every request.

  A login refused before the user is known writes no audit row. It
  writes one WARNING line instead (audit.py:log_refused_login):

    login_failed                wrong name or password
    login_rate_limited          /auth/login over the rate limit
    mfa_rate_limited            /auth/mfa/verify over the rate limit
    backup_code_rate_limited    /auth/totp/backup over the rate limit

  The line carries the client IP and, for the first two, the name the
  client sent, JSON-quoted so it cannot start a new line. The password
  is never written. Anyone can repeat these attempts, so they stay
  out of auth.db. A wrong TOTP or backup code comes after a correct
  password and stays in the audit log (mfa_failed,
  backup_code_failed): it tells the operator that someone holds the
  password.

  docker-compose.yml rotates the container log: json-file driver,
  three files of 10 MB at most.


Configuration
-------------

  All settings are environment-driven, loaded once at startup into
  an immutable AuthConfig (config.py, frozen=True, slots=True). The
  variables and their defaults:

    AUTH_HOST / AUTH_PORT        0.0.0.0 / 8443
    AUTH_LOG_LEVEL               info      (uvicorn and phantom-auth)
    AUTH_PROXY_URL               unix:///var/run/phantom/daemon.sock
                                 (or http://...); UDS in production
    AUTH_PROXY_TIMEOUT           30.0      (seconds)
    AUTH_DB_DIR                  /var/lib/phantom/auth
    AUTH_SECRETS_DIR             /run/secrets  (Docker secrets path)
    AUTH_TOKEN_LIFETIME          86400     (24h access token)
    AUTH_INACTIVITY_TIMEOUT      1800      (30m inactivity timeout)
    AUTH_MFA_TOKEN_LIFETIME      120       (2m MFA token)
    AUTH_TOTP_SETUP_LIFETIME     300       (5m TOTP setup window)
    AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY          65536     (64 KB)
    AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY   67108864  (64 MB, backup routes)
    PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4 / _V6      172.28.0.0/24 / fd00:d0ce::/64
                                 (phantom-net subnets, trusted for
                                 X-Forwarded-For)
    AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES         empty (further networks trusted for
                                 X-Forwarded-For)
    AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW/MAX   60 / 5


Error envelope
--------------

  Every JSON response, success or failure, uses the same shape:

    Success:  { "ok": true,  "data": ... }
    Error:    { "ok": false, "error_code": "..." }

  Error codes are stable strings (TOKEN_EXPIRED, SESSION_INACTIVE,
  SUPERADMIN_REQUIRED, BODY_TOO_LARGE, …; the full list is
  docs/API_CODES.md). HTTP status codes remain meaningful, but the
  SPA branches on error_code.

  Three layers:
    auth_service/errors.py        Python-side hierarchy: AuthError →
                                  AuthSecretsError, AuthDatabaseError,
                                  AuthTokenError → AuthTokenExpiredError,
                                  AuthTokenInvalidError.
    ApiException                  HTTP-aware exception carrying
                                  status + error_code.
    app.py:_register_error_handlers
                                  Maps HTTPException, ApiException
                                  among them, and FastAPI's
                                  RequestValidationError into the
                                  envelope. An uncaught exception
                                  answers a plain-text 500.


Bootstrap
---------

  The first setup runs tools/setup.sh and tools/bootstrap.py, once
  per host. setup.sh builds phantom-auth:latest from the Dockerfile
  when it is missing and runs bootstrap.py once in a --rm container.
  bootstrap.py:

    1. Generates an Ed25519 keypair via PyNaCl SigningKey.
    2. Writes signing/verify keys as 64-char hex files into
       $AUTH_SECRETS_DIR.
    3. Creates auth.db with the full schema (kept in sync with
       auth_service/db/schema.sql).
    4. Generates a 32-character random password for the initial
       admin user.
    5. Hashes it with Argon2id and inserts the user with role
       'superadmin'.
    6. Writes the password to $AUTH_SECRETS_DIR/.admin_password;
       setup.sh sets it to mode 600 with the two key files. The
       output names the file and prints both keys as [REDACTED].

  After bootstrap, the runtime container opens the existing DB and
  keys; startup aborts when either is missing.

  Further superadmin accounts come from the host, never from the API
  (POST /auth/users creates admin accounts only).
  tools/add_superadmin.py runs in the auth image, started by the
  daemon's setup tools: it reads the password on stdin and from
  nowhere else, applies the rules of CreateUserRequest, and writes a
  user_created row to the audit log. It prints one result line and
  never the password.


Container shape
---------------

  Dockerfile         debian:trixie-slim pinned by digest, packages
                     upgraded at build time, Debian's python3 with a
                     venv at /opt/venv, apt and the venv tooling
                     purged in a pruned stage, then requirements.txt.
                     CMD runs `python -m auth_service`. The image
                     carries no source: the package scripts/packager.py
                     builds puts the code in services/auth-service of
                     the daemon repository, and the daemon stack
                     mounts its auth_service directory read-only (see
                     DISTRIBUTION).
  docker-compose.yml Mounts the Ed25519 keypair as Docker secrets,
                     restart: unless-stopped, container log rotated
                     (json-file, 3 x 10 MB). Sets the server, proxy,
                     token and rate-limit variables explicitly.
                     AUTH_DB_DIR, AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY,
                     AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES and PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4/_V6
                     are not in the file; the container takes their
                     config.py defaults. AUTH_SECRETS_DIR in the file
                     only names where the key files live on the host;
                     inside the container the default /run/secrets
                     applies, and Docker secrets are mounted there.

  The runtime container writes auth.db: sessions, the audit log,
  users and TOTP state. The daemon stack mounts container-data/auth-db
  read-write.


Source layout
-------------

  auth_service/
    __init__.py           __version__ — single source of truth
    __main__.py           uvicorn entry, service logger config
    app.py                FastAPI factory + lifespan + middleware
    config.py             env-driven AuthConfig
    audit.py              audit_log, account form, refused-login log line
    errors.py             exception hierarchy
    models.py             pydantic request/response types
    crypto/
      jwt.py              Ed25519 JWT encode + decode
      keys.py             Ed25519 key load + validate
      password.py         Argon2id hash + verify
      totp.py             RFC 6238 + backup codes
    db/
      schema.sql          auth.db DDL
      connection.py       open_connection: pragmas, schema, migration
      repository.py       AuthDB (every query), resolve_db_path,
                          open_auth_db
    middleware/
      auth.py             require_auth + require_superadmin
      rate_limit.py       sliding window limiter
    routes/
      __init__.py         router registration
      proxy.py            /api/* reverse proxy
      auth/
        __init__.py       /auth router prefix
        login.py          /login, /mfa/verify, /totp/backup
        session.py        /logout, /me
        password.py       /password/verify, /password/change
        totp.py           /totp/setup, /totp/confirm, /totp/disable
        users.py          /users, /users/{name}, /users/{name}/password
        audit.py          /audit (superadmin)
        _helpers.py       shared route helpers
